dma: DCOL is twelve bits in 2D mode, not eight

2D mode splits the DMA counter 12/12 (DSP56300FM ch.10), and DCOH was already
extracted as DCO[23:12], so masking DCOL with 0xff contradicted it. A DCO of
0x23F loaded 0x3F, so the channel wrapped after 64 words instead of 576 and
then subtracted its (negative) offset register every 64 words, walking the
destination cursor down out of its buffer and over unrelated memory.

diff --git a/source/dsp56kEmu/dma.cpp b/source/dsp56kEmu/dma.cpp
index bd3e603..abafa59 100644
--- a/source/dsp56kEmu/dma.cpp
+++ b/source/dsp56kEmu/dma.cpp
@@ -156,7 +156,16 @@ namespace dsp56k
 		else
 		{
 			m_dcohInit = m_dco >> 12;
-			m_dcolInit = m_dco & 0xff;
+			// DCOL is TWELVE bits, not eight. DCOH is extracted as DCO[23:12] just
+			// above, so a 0xff mask here contradicts it: 2D mode splits DCO 12/12
+			// (DSP56300FM ch.10). With 0xff a DCO of 0x23F loaded 0x3F, so the channel
+			// took its wrap branch after 64 words instead of 576 and then SUBTRACTED
+			// the (negative) DOR every 64 words, walking the destination cursor DOWN
+			// out of its buffer. Observed on the Octatrack's ESAI receive channel
+			// (DCO3 = 0x23F, DOR3 = -575): audio samples landed on the DSP payload's
+			// module pointer table in low X memory and the core jumped through one.
+			// The transmit channel hid it -- DCO2 = 0xFF fits in eight bits.
+			m_dcolInit = m_dco & 0xfff;
 
 			m_dcoh = m_dcohInit;
 			m_dcol = m_dcolInit;
